;; FH-BEHAVIOR-010 — ownership routing for an imperative SDK that owns its own
;; DOM subtree: ONE idempotent reclamation path, entered from either side.
;;
;; The Google Maps JavaScript API is the shape this row exists for, and it is
;; the hardest of the three third-party shapes because the library owns things
;; the substrate normally owns. `new google.maps.Map(node, opts)` takes the node
;; over — it builds and rebuilds a subtree React never sees — and it runs its own
;; render loop. An `OverlayView` goes further: its `onAdd` hands the author a
;; PANE NODE the LIBRARY created, and the only way to put a Freehand view in it
;; is an explicit nested root. And reclamation can be initiated from EITHER side:
;; the author's `:disconnect`, or the library's own removal callback firing
;; because the user, the map, or a control took the overlay away.
;;
;; Two initiators and one set of resources is where an integration leaks. This
;; row states the routing that does not: the behavior owns the root identity, and
;; the two initiators share ONE reclamation path that is a no-op after the first
;; entry — so host-removal-then-disconnect, disconnect-then-host-removal, and
;; repeated reclamation all end in the same absence.
;;
;; WHAT RUNS, AND WHAT DOES NOT. The proof mounts a surrogate that reproduces the
;; Maps API's OWNERSHIP SHAPE — construction over a caller's node, a void-returning
;; `setOptions`, a listener handle released by `.remove()`, an `OverlayView` whose
;; `onAdd` supplies a host-created pane and whose `onRemove` is the host-removal
;; callback — and nothing of Google's code. That is deliberate and it is stated
;; here rather than implied: `google.maps` is loaded from the network under an API
;; key against a billed account, so a CI lane cannot execute it, and a proof that
;; pretended otherwise would be measuring a network. The law below is FREEHAND's —
;; who owns the node, who owns the update, and what survives teardown — and it is
;; the surrogate's job to present the same ownership boundary the real library
;; presents, not to be it. See `:evidence :limits`.
{:fh/id "FH-BEHAVIOR-010"
 :fh/law
 "An imperative SDK that owns its own DOM subtree is routed to a registered behavior, which owns the root identity: the connection's memory is one mutable cell, a host-created pane receives an EXPLICIT nested Freehand root, and reclamation is ONE idempotent path entered from both the host-removal callback and `:disconnect` — so either order, and any number of repetitions, leave zero maps, zero listeners, zero overlays and zero nested roots, while the substrate's own connection table and target index are already empty at the instant of unmount."

 ;; What one committed connection owns. `:roots` counts the DOOR's registry, and
 ;; it reads TWO because that is the honest number: the page's own root, and the
 ;; island in the pane the library created. A pane the LIBRARY made is not a
 ;; container Freehand chose, so putting a view in it is an explicit second root
 ;; rather than a portal Freehand does not have (Spec 004 §No neutral portal) —
 ;; and the registry counting it is what makes the island visible to the door's
 ;; own bookkeeping instead of being a tree nobody owns.
 :connected
 {:maps 1 :listeners 1 :overlays 1 :roots 2}

 ;; The reclamation orders. `:entered` is every entry into the ONE path, in
 ;; order; `:did-work` is the entries that actually released anything. The
 ;; difference between the two vectors IS the idempotence — a path that released
 ;; twice would show two, and a path that released nothing would show none.
 :reclamation
 [{:case    "disconnect alone: the author initiates, and the library's own teardown re-enters through the host-removal callback"
   :entered [:disconnect :host-removal]
   :did-work [:disconnect]}
  {:case    "the HOST removes the overlay first — the user, a control, or the map took it away — and `:disconnect` arrives afterwards to find nothing left to do"
   :entered [:host-removal :disconnect]
   :did-work [:host-removal]}
  ;; Note the SECOND entry in this row: it is not the second `:manual`. Releasing
  ;; the map detaches the overlay, which is the library calling the author back
  ;; — so the first entry re-enters the path from the other side before it has
  ;; returned. Re-entrancy is the case a fence written as "have I run yet?"
  ;; after the release would get wrong, and it is why the phase is written
  ;; BEFORE anything is released rather than after.
  {:case    "repeated reclamation: the same path entered twice more by hand after it has already run, plus the library's own re-entry from inside the first release"
   :entered [:manual :host-removal :manual :disconnect]
   :did-work [:manual]}]

 ;; Acceptance 1's absence, and every book reads an EXACT ZERO rather than a
 ;; threshold. The first three are the LIBRARY's own books — it knows what it
 ;; still holds — and the last three are the substrate's.
 :after-reclamation
 {:maps 0 :listeners 0 :overlays 0 :roots 0 :connections 0 :targets []}

 ;; The gap Spec 004 §Commit-only connection and total cleanup states in prose:
 ;; "React will not unmount a nested root from inside a render, so a behavior
 ;; that opened one defers the unmount and its second tree is still open at the
 ;; moment the substrate's own absence is already true."
 ;;
 ;; It is measured here rather than trusted, because it is the one place an
 ;; author's leak check can be written against the wrong clock: a check that
 ;; reads the author's own host resources at the instant the substrate's books
 ;; go empty reads them one task too early, and reports a leak that is not one.
 ;; `:roots` is the door's registry again, so the two rows read as one sentence:
 ;; at unmount the PAGE's root claim is already gone and the island in the host's
 ;; pane is still standing; one task later the author's own release has taken it.
 :the-deferral-gap
 {:at-unmount     {:connections 0 :targets [] :roots 1}
  :one-task-later {:connections 0 :targets [] :roots 0}}

 ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 ;; The roster record (rf2-drpa3.182.7)
 ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 :fh/record
 {:source [re-frame.freehand.behaviors re-frame.freehand.root]

  ;; NO structural tier, for FH-BEHAVIOR-003's reason: on the structural host a
  ;; behavior is an inert marker that connects nothing, so there is no ownership
  ;; to route and no reclamation to observe. The index row binds this law to
  ;; `interpreted host:google-maps` for exactly that reason.
  :mounted
  [{:ns  re-frame.freehand.ownership-maps-dom-cljs-test
    :law "A behavior owning a Maps-shaped SDK reclaims through ONE idempotent path entered from both the host-removal callback and `:disconnect`: either order and any repetition end with zero maps, zero listeners, zero overlays and zero nested Freehand roots, and the nested root's deferred release is measured across the task boundary rather than assumed."}]

  :evidence
  {:reads   [:behaviors/connections :behaviors/targets :root/live-root-ids]
   :counts  [:maps/live :maps/listeners :maps/overlays]
   :residue :none

   ;; The four comparative counts. They are STATIC properties of the route,
   ;; obtained by READING the declarations in the mounted proof — not a runtime
   ;; measurement, and nothing asserts them. They are published so the three
   ;; ownership routes can be compared rather than described; they are not a
   ;; score and not a threshold, and a route is not better for being smaller.
   ;;
   ;; The definitions are written ONCE, for all three witnesses together, in
   ;; `docs/core/freehand/host/ownership-routing.md` §"What the counts count".
   ;; One copy, because three definitions in three files is three incomparable
   ;; numbers. This row's own arithmetic, name by name:
   ;;
   ;;   :exports    1 — `maps-canvas`, one `v/defbehavior`. `overlay-legend` is
   ;;                   NOT counted: it is the view the author chose to mount in
   ;;                   the pane, application code the route did not add.
   ;;   :crossings  2 — the behavior's `:config` channel, and the explicit nested
   ;;                   root `v/mount`ed into the node the library made. The two
   ;;                   `:commands` positions are NOT counted: they exist so a
   ;;                   case can enter reclamation from a chosen side, which is
   ;;                   the measurement rather than the route. The library's own
   ;;                   removal callback is not a crossing either — it is
   ;;                   registered with the LIBRARY, and it is glue.
   ;;   :glue-sites 4 — `reclaim!`, `:connect`, `:update`, `:disconnect`. Each is
   ;;                   irreducible: the SDK's setter is void so someone must
   ;;                   call it, the instance must be constructed and released,
   ;;                   and two initiators over one set of resources is what
   ;;                   makes the shared path a fourth body rather than a line.
   ;;   :glue-lines 27 — the non-blank, non-comment, non-docstring lines of those
   ;;                   four forms: 9 + 14 + 3 + 1. The suite's own `entries` /
   ;;                   `worked` bookkeeping is excluded for the same reason the
   ;;                   commands are.
   ;;
   ;; This is the most expensive of the three routes by an order of magnitude,
   ;; and that is the finding rather than a fault: it is what "the library owns
   ;; the node" costs, and the route is still the cheapest way to pay it.
   :comparative
   {:exports 1 :crossings 2 :glue-sites 4 :glue-lines 27
    :method  :read-from-source}

   ;; Stated in the record, not only in prose, so a reader of the roster sees
   ;; the boundary without opening the suite. The first two entries bound the
   ;; PROOF; the third states what the ROUTE permanently loses, which is the
   ;; other half of the same honesty and is not recoverable by any recipe.
   :limits  ["Google's `google.maps` code is NOT executed: it loads from the network under an API key against a billed account, which no CI lane can do. The mounted proof drives a surrogate reproducing the API's ownership shape — node takeover, void-returning `setOptions`, a listener handle released by `.remove()`, an `OverlayView` supplying a host-created pane and calling back on removal — so what is proven is Freehand's ownership routing at that boundary, not Google's implementation of it."
             "No npm dependency was added for this row. A real `@googlemaps/js-api-loader` in the browser lane would make the gate a test of a network."
             "WHAT THE ROUTE PERMANENTLY LOSES, stated beside what it proves. CONTEXT: the nested root in the library's own pane is an ISLAND — it does not inherit the outer tree's React context, its Suspense boundaries, or its event bubbling, and nothing here pretends it does; app-db is shared regardless, because a frame is not a tree. EVIDENCE: the SDK's subtree is a LEAF to Freehand — no structural render, nothing in any manifest, and nothing inside it visible to the substrate's own bookkeeping. SSR: none for either tree — a behavior connects only on commit, so a server render emits the empty node and the island does not exist at all until the client mounts. None of the three is recoverable; each is the price of letting the library own the node."]}

  :prose :executable}}
